Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:06.26,Title,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,445)\fad(600,500)\bord3\blur0.5}ODYSSEY — BOOK I
Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:06.26,Deck,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,565)\fad(600,500)}Athena comes to Ithaca;\NTelemachus resolves to seek his father.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.26,0:01:38.15,Header,,0,0,0,,{\fad(800,500)}HOMER’S ODYSSEY  ·  BOOK I
Dialogue: 1,0:00:06.26,0:00:09.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Tell me the man, Muse —\Nthe man of many turnings,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:09.66,0:00:14.70,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}who was driven wandering far, once he had sacked\NTroy’s holy citadel:
Dialogue: 1,0:00:14.70,0:00:18.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}many were the cities of men he saw,\Nand he learned their minds,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:18.26,0:00:21.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}many the griefs he suffered at heart\Non the open sea,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:21.30,0:00:25.06,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}striving to win his own life\Nand his companions’ homecoming.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:25.06,0:00:28.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Even so he could not save them,\Nhard though he strove:
Dialogue: 1,0:00:28.84,0:00:31.24,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}they perished by their own recklessness,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:31.24,0:00:34.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}fools, who ate the cattle\Nof Helios Hyperion,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:34.64,0:00:37.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and the god took from them\Ntheir day of homecoming.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:37.36,0:00:41.70,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Begin where you will, goddess, daughter of Zeus —\Ntell us, too, the tale.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:41.70,0:00:44.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}By then all the others,\Nall who had escaped sheer ruin,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:44.80,0:00:48.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}were home, safely past the war\Nand past the sea.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:48.88,0:00:51.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Him alone — aching for homecoming\Nand his wife —
Dialogue: 1,0:00:51.08,0:00:55.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the queenly nymph Calypso,\Nshining among goddesses, held back
Dialogue: 1,0:00:55.32,0:00:58.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}in her hollow caves,\Nhungry to make him her husband.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:58.56,0:01:01.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But when the year came,\Nin the wheeling round of seasons,
Dialogue: 1,0:01:01.88,0:01:05.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}in which the gods had spun the thread\Nof his return home to Ithaca —
Dialogue: 1,0:01:05.54,0:01:09.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}not even there was he free of trials,\Neven among his own.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:09.26,0:01:13.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And the gods pitied him, all but Poseidon:\Nhe raged without rest
Dialogue: 1,0:01:13.84,0:01:17.42,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}against godlike Odysseus,\Nuntil the day he reached his own land.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:17.42,0:01:21.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But now Poseidon was gone\Namong the far-off Ethiopians —
Dialogue: 1,0:01:21.66,0:01:25.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the Ethiopians, sundered in two,\Nremotest of mankind,
Dialogue: 1,0:01:25.96,0:01:28.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}half where Hyperion sets\Nand half where he rises —
Dialogue: 1,0:01:28.94,0:01:34.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to receive a hecatomb of bulls and rams,\Nand there he sat at the feast, delighting.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:34.22,0:01:38.15,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But the other gods were gathered together\Nin the halls of Olympian Zeus,
